#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "8337af7a-73e8-5ae2-9bed-b03f193b2950" :question/math-academy-id "e-21657" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6feea8db62000e44334251a96c39bd8d0d8d7b1ec9a61380f3a5b4b613ea6c80.png)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/bceb494a6baeca8695157b4fd285b2880a242367747c0d72a0f573612ef87652.png)\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b542bd66-f4f8-5b4e-a3e7-4717827c427a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "7b4f569b-ad3a-5a93-a53e-27a186b7700e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21658" :question/problem "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda = a \\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Provide an interpretation of the portrait and determine the possible values of $a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6d13d0dfadac45d40b74cb7e740a880305098f0939e2f45d74706c4022183c54.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166484 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "d7b40689-7b7e-546e-9b66-30e6a95f3ee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21659" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1119df1d-1f7d-5971-833f-0a350a91ed69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362675} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e0fd7c-e2d4-5b1b-9023-664a59fab9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362676} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c01d4a-8af3-5ac1-9e76-fdbae58fd946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362677} {:answer/id #uuid "76f75268-8738-586b-b005-8f540c5f4dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e0fd7c-e2d4-5b1b-9023-664a59fab9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362676} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2fd7ec2-d2e4-52e5-9a73-e5d8bfb8f05b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd267c35-d8da-5404-8602-d9e6ef41833e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362680} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd65a79-97e3-57b3-9957-672d088a4b51" :answer/type {:d