\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},5]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3.54$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab891a150c13ffa780.png)\n\nThe curve is \n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe {{field-8}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/0678458809cf39967d7dda0d1ffbe4d43541ec309266bd08da6245ec23f6439d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3.54$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab891a150c13ffa780.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is $\\text{bounded}.$ So, we have $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/0678458809cf39967d7dda0d1ffbe4d43541ec309266bd08da6245ec23f6439d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e169bbe4-3b25-5ee6-830b-19d9af5dd2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362708} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5ab240-475c-5fb5-8e29-bc13fc3dfa4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362709} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb3ead0-88f5-5c37-8571-adf692c99f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362710} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c58bbc-5cc1-574d-9efc-82dc0571cd71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "