} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362757} {:answer/id #uuid "11c79851-572d-5dec-80e5-d93d14add5e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11c79851-572d-5dec-80e5-d93d14add5e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362758} :answer-field/id #uuid "4375e113-b89b-5799-920f-a420aa39a4c0" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdaf0272-6965-5562-9bf7-1833f93c4524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362760} {:answer/id #uuid "9391d1b0-9c49-5c3a-bb73-64a2d1dd4b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdaf0272-6965-5562-9bf7-1833f93c4524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362760} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5079f80-63b7-5013-8de5-f70039bf557a" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec33219-6e0e-5bbf-a1af-0814511c1a80" :question/math-academy-id "q-334279" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/4be4e2001c6c2f499ea95b932ba600772827ca5ce4df2abedb629ac5d4daee75.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-5}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\nHence, \n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b542bd66-f4f8-5b4e-a3e7-4717827c427a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbdb4242-e289-599d-b91c-c1c65288b6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164487} {:answer/id #uuid "1e66c076-3feb-5cda-9118-e6d6aa331b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a spiral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "